Teach libxml2 2.9.x's ancient config.sub the Apple mobile triplets, and drop
two version-script entries that cannot exist on our targets.

meta.yaml routes this file to any version starting "2.9." -- in practice 2.9.8,
the pin lxml 5.x has to keep, since 5.x does not compile against 2.15 -- and
everything newer to mobile-2.15.x.patch. This is the original November 2024
lxml-enablement patch (PR #11), renamed when PR #48 introduced the
version-suffixed layout.

Hunk 1 -- config.sub. build.sh configures with `--host=$HOST_TRIPLET`, and
2.9.8's config.sub predates Apple's arm64/iOS naming, so it rejects the triplet
before configure starts. This vintage of config.sub splits its argument into a
basic_machine plus an os beginning with `-`, so `arm64-apple-ios` arrives as
machine `arm64-apple` / os `-ios`, and `x86_64-apple-ios-simulator` as machine
`x86_64-apple-ios` / os `-simulator`. The new case matches both machine shapes
(`arm64-apple` and `*-ios`), completes the field with `-unknown`, and forces
`os=-none` -- which is already in the accepted-os list further down, so that
second list never has to be touched. The canonical name produced is not
meaningful beyond that: all configure needs is a triple that parses and that
differs from the build machine's, which is what makes the build register as a
cross build. Real targeting comes from CC/-isysroot, which forge sets.

  (The same-era libxslt patch, mobile-1.1.32.patch, solves the identical
  problem by the other route: it adds the two machine spellings to the long
  list of accepted bare machine names and separately whitelists `-simulator`
  alongside `-ios` in `case $os`. Same effect; the two files were evidently
  written independently.)

Hunk 2 -- libxml2.syms. That file is the ELF version script; 2.9.x hands it to
the linker via --version-script whenever the linker advertises GNU
compatibility, which the NDK's lld does and Apple's ld64 does not, so this hunk
is inert on the iOS legs and matters on Android.

`xmlDllMain` is defined only under HAVE_WIN32_THREADS. On Android it is
therefore named by the version script but never defined, and a modern lld --
which defaults to --no-undefined-version -- fails the link over it. Commenting
it out removes it from the export list (`#` is a comment in a version script;
upstream already uses it for the section headings in this same file).

The ten `docb*` entries in the same hunk are NOT explained, and the commit that
made the change records no reason. Checked against upstream: a default 2.9.8
configure -- which is what build.sh runs -- leaves `--with-docbook` on, so
DOCBparser.c is compiled and all ten symbols are defined under
LIBXML_DOCB_ENABLED. On that reading they should not have needed commenting
out, and the most likely explanation is that they were collateral while hunting
the xmlDllMain failure. Treat this half as unexplained rather than trusting a
rationale; it is at worst harmless, since it only withholds deprecated
DocBook-SGML entry points from libxml2's exports and nothing in the flet tree
calls them.

PR #48 dropped hunk 2 entirely for 2.15.x: upstream removed libxml2.syms around
2.10, so there is no version script left to edit.

